WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.120
[CICADAS]

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.480
I want to ask you what part
of the country you come from.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.600
I originate from Canada.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.920
Now, guess where that is.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.400
I know where Canada is.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.840
I've been there myself.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.400
Well-traveled for a slave.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.080
Solomon Northup is an
excellent player on the violin.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.840
I was born a free man, lived
with my family in New York.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.840
Be good for you ma.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.560
Until the day I was deceived--

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.720
To Solomon.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.520
--kidnapped, sold into slavery.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.240
Well, boy, how you feel now?

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.960
My name is Solomon Northup.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.280
I'm a free man, and you have no
right whatsoever to detain me.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.480
You no free man.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.680
You're nothing but
a Georgia runaway.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.520
[MUSIC - TOPSY CHAPMAN, "ROLL
 JORDAN ROLL"]

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.560
(SINGING) Went down
to the river Jordan.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:54.840
And that servant--

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.880
Who don't obey his lord--

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.800
--shall be beaten
with many strikes.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.400
That's scripture.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.440
The condition of your
laborers, it's all wrong.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.280
They're my property.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.360
You say that with pride.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.480
I say it is fact.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:08.160
Snake!

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.000
Man does how he pleases
with his property.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.160
Who?

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.160
You come here.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.320
I said come here.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.400
Days ago, I was with
my family, in my home.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.920
Now you tell me all is lost.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.160
If you want to survive, do
and say as little as possible.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.840
(SINGING) My soul
arise in heaven, Lord.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.120
Well, I don't want to survive.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.800
(SINGING) For the
year when Jordan roll.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.680
I want to live.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.600
I thought you knowed something.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.800
I did as instructed.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.560
There's something wrong, it's
wrong with the instruction.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.160
Master brought you here to work.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.400
Anymore will earn
you 100 lashes.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.080
I know what it's like to be
the object of Master's lashes.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.920
In his own time, could
learn to manage them all.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.400
I will survive.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.400
I will not fall into despair.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.160
I will keep myself hardy
till freedom is opportune.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:05.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.480
I was a free man.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:09.880
I'm not a slave.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.800
 Hello, everyone, and welcome.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.520
My name is Steve McQueen.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.440
I'm a director,
writer, producer.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.400
And I'm here to talk to an
amazing individual, an artist,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.160
a cinematographer, Sean Bobbitt.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:41.800
Hello, Sean.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:42.760
How are you?

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.560
Hi, Steve.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.040
Always great to see you.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.000
Good, good, good.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.880
I've just seen your last film,
Judas and the Black Messiah,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.320
that you shot.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.720
It's extraordinary.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.120
I just want to ask you--

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.480
the first question,
really, is, how is it--

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.840
how do you begin to
collaborate with a director?

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.000
What is your first process?

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.680
 
 
77
00:03:01,610 --&gt; 00:03:07,670
The process of collaboration is
actually what it's all about.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:17.320
The job of the
cinematographer is

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.920
to interpret the
ideas of the director

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.040
and put them on a screen.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.680
So the first thing that you
need to do when you first

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.680
meet a director on
a project is, first

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.280
of all, determine whether you
want to work with them or not.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.960
And this is-- the answer to that
is yes, then you kind of need

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.080
to determine what your
role is with the master

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.000
cinematographer.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.800
Directors are all
individually unique,

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.640
and they all have their
own means of communication,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.400
their own ideas.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.040
And some of them
are very, very good

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.800
at getting those
ideas across, in terms

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.120
of their understanding
of the film and the story

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:12.560
and the real depth of the tale
that they're trying to tell.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.280
And some of them are very,
very powerfully visual.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:19.120
And they have worked on
this project for years,

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.440
whereas I come in off
the street, literally.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:28.440
And some of them,
the visual power

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.120
means that my role is
slightly different.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.160
If they have strong ideas,
then all I have to do

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.480
is listen to those
ideas very carefully

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.800
and try to ensure
that those ideas are

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.040
what are incorporated into
the visual look of the film.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.160
Some directors are not
so strong visually.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.400
Their interests are
more in performance,

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.240
are more in
storytelling, and less

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.440
to do with the actual frame.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.040
And for those people,
then it's my job

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.440
to start to suggest
ideas, to put things

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.400
forward as to what the visual
approach of the film might be.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.800
And so all the time, you're
trying to develop a dialogue,

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.600
and, from that dialogue, discern
you know what this film is

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.920
and what it means,
and then try to--

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.280
between the collaboration
of the two of you,

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.080
and also with all
the other heads

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.000
of departments, particularly the
production designer, costume,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.200
hair and makeup, and
sound, working all together

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.920
to try and make those
ideas real and to put them

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.920
on a screen in a coherent
way that effectively

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.720
tells the story.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.440
What is the most
challenging aspect

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.280
of being a cinematographer?

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.840
Is there something that you meet
every time you go on a shoot?

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.800
Or is there something which
sort of you know is coming

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.920
and you have to overcome
it, or something like that?

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.880
What is the most challenging
aspect of doing your job?

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.760
Well, it's an odd
job in many ways,

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:18.800
because the actual
description is very broad.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:28.960
A cinematographer, it's not
just about pointing a camera

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.840
at someone and
turning a light on.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:39.240
The side of it that is always
unknown is sort of the--

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.880
 really, it's management
and logistics.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:46.880
Yes.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:53.480
Because you-- a film
runs on momentum.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.400
And you don't want
to be the person who

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.400
is slowing down that momentum.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.920
So you have to be prepared
and you have to be planned.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:07.280
And you have to be ready
for just about anything that

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.480
might happen.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:11.560
And I have to say, the most
exciting things that I really

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:15.880
enjoy on a shoot are when
the unexpected does happen.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.280
I was about to ask
you about that.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.360
How do you deal with that?

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.840
I think your background
is a documentary film

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.240
cameraman is sort
of, I think, one

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:31.000
of your jewels in
your crown, really.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.320
Yeah.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:34.160
Coming from the world
of documentaries,

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.520
where you don't have a
script, where there's

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.840
no linear narrative, that as
you're working on a project,

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.400
it's happening in front of
you, and you have to respond

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:47.840
in real time, when
you're working in drama

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.720
you have a script.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.240
You know what's
supposed to happen.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.560
And so you can plan accordingly.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.560
But at the same time,
one thing that I've

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.000
learned over the years, and
particularly working with you,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.200
Steve, is to expect
the unexpected,

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.720
and to look forward
to the unexpected,

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.280
and to embrace the unexpected.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:13.040
Embrace the unexpected, exactly.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.280
Because it's in those
things that you could never

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:21.120
imagine happening that there's
little moments of magic.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.120
At what point-- I mean,
imagine all the people

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:25.160
who are interested in
being a cinematographer.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.800
At what point were
you comfortable?

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.240
At what point-- how long did it
take you to get to that moment,

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.760
or if you ever got to that
moment, where you are on set,

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:37.960
and there's a certain
understanding or--

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:39.120
I won't even say confidence.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.080
I don't-- but there's a certain
kind of-- when things just came

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:42.800
together.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:44.760
Was there a time when
things just came together

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:50.720
and you knew you didn't
feel that anxiety, as such?

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:52.720
Was there a time?

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:54.880
I'm still looking for it.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.840
In fact, I don't
ever want to find it.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.560
Mm-hmm.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.720
Because if you're not just
that little bit afraid

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:08.400
at the beginning of every film,
then you become complacent,

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.840
and there's no space for it.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:12.000
We're incredibly privileged.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.440
We're paid to make films.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.920
And so with that privilege
comes a responsibility,

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.800
and that responsibility
is to make those films

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.040
as best as you possibly can.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.560
And I believe that,
as a cinematographer,

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.320
the exploration of the
art of cinematography,

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:35.600
the art of filmmaking, the art
of storytelling never ends.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.120
You never know everything,
and you should never

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.960
become complacent.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.440
When did you know that you
wanted to be a cameraman?

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.480
When did you know that
you didn't want to direct,

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:48.840
when you knew you
didn't want to do sound?

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.080
I want to put this
thing on my eye

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:52.880
and I want to sort
of start shooting?

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.160
Was it the image?

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.440
Well, it wasn't that long ago.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:05.200
For many years, through
circumstance more than desire--

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:06.720
you know, I always
thought I'd would

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.760
be a writer-director
when I was young,

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.800
and that's what I
was going to do.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:12.040
How did you get to that point?

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:13.920
When did film catch you as well?

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.600
Well, I had been a news
cameraman for about 10 years,

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:20.440
and then I was a documentary
cameraman for about 10 years.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:25.720
And I was getting kind of
bored with that format.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.400
And very fortunately, I
got fired off of a job,

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:33.240
the only job I've ever been
fired off of, and took that

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.960
time and opportunity-- and I
was really very upset as well--

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.360
to go off to do a cinematography
seminar at the International

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.800
Film and Television
School of Rockport, Maine,

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.320
which was being given
by Billy Williams.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.240
And I was there for a week.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.600
And after the third day,
I suddenly thought, wow,

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:54.800
I actually--

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:58.120
I need to be a cinematographer.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:10:59.200
Forget about writing.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:01.000
Forget about directing.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.400
I love this.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:03.320
What was that thought?

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.320
Why did you need to be
a DP, cinematographer?

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:06.880
Why?

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:08.640
I don't know.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.120
I think for so many
years I was in denial.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:14.120
I loved being a cameraman.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:21.160
And I had shot for so many
years, always in the belief

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.640
that I was going to
be something else.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.400
And what Billy
Williams managed to do

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.600
was to put together a lot of
disparate things in my mind

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:36.320
about being a cameraman,
about being a cinematographer.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.280
And things clicked, not
just technically, but also

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:44.120
emotionally, in a way
that was unexpected.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:45.560
Was it story?

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:46.440
It must have been--

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.080
I'm thinking it might
have to be story.

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:49.080
I don't know.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:53.080
Well, that was a very big part
of it, this sudden realization

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:58.720
that, OK, if you're
working in drama,

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.200
there are things that you
can do with the camera,

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:06.480
with the lighting,
with movement, with all

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.640
of the aspects of
cinematography that

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:13.360
tell the story in a different
way, because up to that point,

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.080
I had been reactive.

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:17.440
When you're doing
news documentaries,

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.800
you're simply reacting
to everything around you.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.040
And suddenly, I was presented
with the possibility

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:23.080
of creation--

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:23.560
Mm-hmm.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:24.080
Control.

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.880
--of the creation of images.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.680
And that was just--

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:36.120
it was overpowering to suddenly
realize that, oh, my God, there

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:39.240
is actually
something to this job

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:42.360
that I had just been doing to
make money for all these years.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:44.640
And then literally, from
the end of that week

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.680
on, that's all I wanted
to do, was to be--

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.680
What was it like, your first
day on set, on a film set,

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.600
on a motion picture film set?

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:55.040
What was that like,
the first day.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:57.960
It was absolutely terrifying.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:12:58.920
It was really--

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:00.320
I thought I was ready.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:02.040
I thought I was really prepared.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.320
And it was like, no, you're not.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:09.240
It was was unlike anything
I'd ever done before.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.120
But it was good terrifying.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:16.680
It was just exciting and
challenging and unexpected.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:18.680
And at the end of
it, it was like, OK,

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:21.040
I know what I'm going to be
doing for the rest of my life

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:25.600
now, so long as
they don't fire me.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:28.800
Is there usually a
certain sense or feeling

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.360
you have when you read a
script for the first time

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.000
on how it could evolve?

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:36.960
What's the first reaction when
you go to read the script?

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.400
You think in images, or not?

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.160
Well, when I first
read a script,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:46.040
I purposely don't have
any images in my head.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.680
I'm purely interested
in the story.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:50.080
Am I interested in the story?

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:51.440
Does the story move me?

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:54.280
And that's the single
most important thing.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:59.800
Is there an emotion that
is elicited by that script?

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.360
If there's no emotion,
then it's kind of,

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:04.440
what's the point of this?

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.560
What is the average viewer
going to see in this?

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:11.040
So it really is the--

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:16.200
it is the story that, on
top of everything else,

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:20.880
is the predicator of whether
I'm interested in a film.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:23.920
And then secondly
is the director

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:29.440
and what their ideas are about
the telling of this story,

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:32.560
because if they
don't have any ideas,

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.760
then where's the
story going to go?

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:41.920
It can't become anything more
than what's written on the page

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.640
without an idea.

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:48.720
So it's really getting--

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.880
again, having an
emotional response

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:57.480
to what the director is saying,
and listening very carefully

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:02.400
to what they have to say,
and thinking, and then

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.680
start to develop the
visual side of things,

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:07.640
because it's the interpretation.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:09.880
It's trying to get into
the director's head--

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:13.080
not that I've ever really tried
to get into your head, Steve.

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.160
It would be much too--

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.680
You're welcome.

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:19.040
I've only got a little head.

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:19.840
I couldn't take it.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:22.200
It couldn't work.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.040
Excuse me?

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:32.120
But it's sort of just trying
to understand what the core is.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:33.720
What's the point of this?

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:34.520
For sure.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:38.360
And then from that point,
trying to then kind of eke out

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:43.920
all the ideas around it
to sort of start to build

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:48.400
an approach, a visual approach,
to the film, slowly but surely,

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:53.760
and working with the director,
looking at films, looking at

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:55.160
photographs.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:56.760
You've sent me poems.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:16:02.120
You've sent me photographic
books, books of Velasquez,

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:04.440
which I still have
and still go over.

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:11.920
All of these things start
to inform an approach.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:14.880
And then it's sort of
bouncing those ideas back

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.920
to the director and getting
steerage, movement, and finding

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.680
which way the film
is going to go.

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:25.760
And like we say, you
get all these ideas.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:26.600
You get an approach.

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:27.920
You get an idea.

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:30.440
You talk through,
hopefully, the key scenes

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:33.080
and have a very strong idea
of what's going to happen.

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:38.360
But then you sit there firmly in
the knowledge that on the day,

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.440
you can just throw it all away.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:41.480
Absolutely.

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:47.480
A few questions-- again,
when you're on set--

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.760
what I very much admire about
cinematography in general

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:51.800
is it's a hard job.

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:56.040
It's a really-- because
you're on your action, cut.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:57.800
From the beginning of
the day, end of day,

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:01.680
everyone's running
off with the DP.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:04.880
How do you stay focused?

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:05.760
I'm fascinated.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:09.440
How do you keep your
concentration 100% all the way

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:10.120
through the day?

00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:12.080
How do you do that?

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:12.880
Or can you?

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:18.240
Well, I think it's practice
more than anything else.

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:23.240
I've had the advantage of
all those years of working

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:27.120
technically with a camera
and lighting and everything

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:29.880
else for so many years
that I don't really

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:33.200
have to think about
that, and so then I

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:36.920
can really focus
on what's happening

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:39.520
at that moment in time.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:43.800
And that's the key, because
it's the only thing that matters

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.560
is that moment in time.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:49.680
And that's why I
operate as well,

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:52.480
because it keeps my focus.

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:55.280
I did one TV series
where I didn't operate,

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.160
and I will never do that again.

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:58.920
I was bored to tears--

00:17:58.920 --> 00:17:59.840
Oh, my god.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:03.560
--and in the end, could care
less about what was going on.

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.000
So I think that's--

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:09.280
--a big part of it, is just
being there and being right

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:15.400
on the front edge of it, which
is also an amazing privilege

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:18.000
because you get to see
those performances,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:19.720
and you're right there.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.480
And it's like, whoa.

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:26.640
And that's a very
regenerative thing.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:31.360
My question-- again, for people
who are interested in being

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:34.800
a cinematographer--

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:36.840
what is the aspect--
again, I always

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.040
start a question with "what."

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.240
But what is the
thing that you feel

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:42.960
that someone needs
to be as a DP,

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:44.760
or at least have a
sort of a sense of?

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:51.360
You have to have, I think,
a very inquisitive mind.

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:56.440
You really need to
want to know things,

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:00.840
because there's so much to
know as a cinematographer.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.040
Forget about all
the technical stuff.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:07.800
It's trying to understand
the story itself,

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:10.920
trying to understand your
relationship with the director,

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:12.640
with all the other
heads of department,

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:15.480
trying to understand your
relationship with the crew

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.400
and how to get the
best out of them.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:20.400
You're forever trying to
understand what's going on.

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:23.360
And constantly, you're always
looking, always looking

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:27.920
and always thinking,
and trying to get better

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:34.080
the whole time, because it's one
of those jobs that never ends.

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:38.480
Every film you do, well, if
you don't learn something new,

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:41.200
then I think you're
doing it wrong,

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.120
because you should
be pushing yourself.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:45.400
You should be pushing your crew.

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:50.560
Everyone around you should
be striving to always become

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.920
better at what they're doing.

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:58.600
So I think the important thing
is to have an inquisitive mind,

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:06.240
to have an open mind, and to
have no ego, because there's

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:10.040
just no space for that
in a cinematographer.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:12.720
One-- actors, tell me about-- of
course, I think a lot of people

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.160
want to know about actors.

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:16.240
And you're looking at the
lens, through the lens.

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:18.440
And of course, director's
looking at whatever.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:22.000
How do you work with actors?

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:23.600
Directors work with
actors, of course,

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:25.000
but you work with actors too.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:26.160
How do you work with actors?

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:27.680
What's your thing?

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:31.160
It's a thing of immense respect.

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:34.280
I think another
big part of my job

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:38.400
is to ensure that the
actors have the space,

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.480
and also, through control of
my crew and everything else

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:46.680
I possibly can, that
there are no distractions.

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:50.560
When they come on to set,
people are respectful.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:51.680
They're quiet.

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:53.560
Everything is organized.

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:58.320
We're ready to go so that they
have all the time they need

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:02.200
with you to find a
performance to begin with,

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:04.600
and then they are not
distracted once we

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:07.480
get into the actual
filming side of it.

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:10.160
So the respect thing is--

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:11.840
there are only two
things that I've ever

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:17.880
failed in terms of the
academic world, and one of them

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:21.240
was acting, and the other
one was theater lighting.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:26.480
Of the British
academic world, yeah.

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:29.600
But I know I can't
do what they do.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:33.640
And I am in awe
of what they do--

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:34.280
Absolutely.

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:37.360
--and the power
that they possess.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:42.280
And so I feel beholden
to do my best to ensure

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:44.920
that they can do their best.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:45.400
Mm-hmm.

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:51.040
The other question
I want to ask you

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:56.720
is people like the script
supervisor, relationship

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:02.160
with that person, the
relationship with the producers

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:02.680
and so forth.

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.120
What's your
relationship with the,

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:05.840
outside of your crew,
the other aspects,

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:07.720
elements of the production?

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:14.920
I think of myself as a
director-cinematographer.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:20.880
And so my main focus of
attention is the director.

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:25.680
But also ancillary to
that, the script supervisor

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:27.240
is absolutely crucial.

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:28.120
Yes.

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:31.080
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:32.800
We love them and we hate them.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:35.680
Yeah, do not ever cross
the script supervisor.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:41.640
But everyone, I think, it
is a matter of respect.

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:43.200
Yes.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:47.800
It's people working
under very close quarters

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:50.920
in very stressful situation.

00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:57.200
So you really have to be as nice
as you can, as much as you can,

00:22:57.200 --> 00:23:00.920
so that you're trying
to keep that stress out.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.200
I don't want to
stress my crew out,

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:05.000
because that'll stress
me out, and it'll

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:06.720
stress everyone else out.

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:10.640
So it's trying to be
calm the whole time, calm

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.240
and differential.

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:17.120
When you need to be firm,
then you're firm, but only

00:23:17.120 --> 00:23:18.440
when you need to.

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:20.480
It's that respect
that one generates

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:23.280
that allows people to
focus, to concentrate,

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:25.400
to take themselves seriously,
to respect themselves

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:27.240
so they don't become sloppy.

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:31.440
And I think that reverberates
throughout the crew,

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:33.400
and it's what you've
obviously said

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:37.640
and I have actually felt on set.

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:43.360
One of the questions I want to
ask you as well is lighting.

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:48.320
For people who want to be
DPs, how difficult is that?

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:50.200
Again, it's one
of those things--

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:52.840
how do you learn about lighting?

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:54.320
You're of master of light.

00:23:54.320 --> 00:23:56.800
How do you-- how--

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:58.720
shooting somebody, and
having to find a frame,

00:23:58.720 --> 00:23:59.480
and then lighting.

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:02.480
Like, there's all these lights
and stuff, and how do I--

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:04.240
where would you start from?

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:05.920
What advice would
you give someone?

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:11.920
Well, people look at lighting
as if it were a black art.

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:13.360
But it's not.

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:17.040
You look around you--
the world is lit.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:18.560
There's natural daylight.

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:19.880
There's moonlight.

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:22.200
And then there are all
the man-made lights

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.200
that we put around us.

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:26.400
Just spend a little
bit of time looking

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:30.560
at the light in every
room that you walk into.

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:32.880
And after a while, you
start to understand.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:37.640
You'll start to see the light
and see what the light's doing.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:41.640
And I think it was
20 years of walking

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:45.480
into rooms as a documentary
and news cameraman.

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:49.040
I walk into a room and I know
exactly what the dominant light

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:52.080
source is, what the color
temperature of that light

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.800
is as well, even if you can't
see the color temperature.

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:57.000
So it's experience.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:59.400
It's like any skill.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.560
But you start with one light.

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:06.680
And in a dark room with
a figure in front of you,

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:08.680
shine that light on it.

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:12.320
And then move that light around,
because it's actually not

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:13.040
about light.

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:15.280
It's about shadows.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:19.280
And it's how you then
manipulate those shadows

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:22.440
to hide or reveal.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:27.360
That's really the initial
key, is understanding that.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:31.400
And a lot of it is
really basic portraiture

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:35.120
and being able to look
at a face and understand

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:38.000
how it's going to
take the light,

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.560
and then plan accordingly
for the whole of the film.

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:44.760
But it's like everything
that is a skill.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:47.120
It is learned over time.

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:49.280
And it's learned
through trial and error

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:52.160
and big, big, big mistakes--

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:55.480
I mean, massive mistakes--

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:56.880
that you're never
going to forget

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:58.400
and you're never
going to do again.

00:25:58.400 --> 00:25:59.320
No.

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:04.880
But if you're not trying to
learn, trying to push it,

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:06.960
trying to understand,
then you're

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.800
never going to make a mistake.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:11.480
And if you never make a mistake,
then you're never going to grow

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:14.160
and you're never going
to truly understand.

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:19.680
It's only through,
I think, humiliation

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:23.240
in many ways that
we can move forward,

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:26.480
even our own self-humiliation,
where we do something wrong.

00:26:26.480 --> 00:26:28.400
But I know a lot of
people, when they first

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:29.920
start with
cinematography, they're

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:34.880
absolutely so afraid
of making a mistake

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:36.960
that they don't do anything.

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:41.960
And you can't progress that way.

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:45.640
So it's kind of a
fundamental of learning.

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:50.440
But lighting is really just
look around you, and really

00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:52.440
start to look.

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:55.600
How do you communicate with
your grips and your gaffers?

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.520
How does that work?

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:00.520
You've had some-- you've got
great relationships with yours.

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:01.680
How did they work?

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.240
How does that work?

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:06.200
Well, when I first
started, I'd never

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:08.160
worked with a grip or a gaffer.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:09.960
I had no idea what they do.

00:27:09.960 --> 00:27:11.760
You had no use for them.

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:13.520
I had no use for them, exactly.

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.880
And I would move lights and
I would do things and move

00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:18.800
tripods on this
set and the other.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:20.680
But I was very fortunate.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:22.720
The people that I
first worked with

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:24.640
were really patient with me.

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:27.840
And they kind of explained
what their jobs were

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:31.000
and what my job was.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:34.720
And because of that, I have--
again, it's about respect.

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:39.960
They have knowledge and
skills that I don't have.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:47.080
So I can take an idea and
explain it to them technically,

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:50.560
because over time, again, that's
another skill you develop,

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:53.040
is the language of filmmaking.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:56.800
And we all-- grips
have a grip language.

00:27:56.800 --> 00:27:58.520
Lighting people have
a lighting language.

00:27:58.520 --> 00:28:01.720
Camera people have a camera
language with its own phrases

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:03.920
and terms and equipments
and everything else.

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:05.960
So as the cinematographer,
you slowly

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:08.360
learn all of those
things so that you

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:11.480
can have those
conversations with people

00:28:11.480 --> 00:28:15.400
and explain what's in
your head in a way.

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:19.680
Communication-- I mean, I should
have said this so much earlier.

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:22.080
Communication is
everything when it

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:28.040
comes to working with a crew,
with a director, with anybody.

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.320
You have to be
able to understand

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:35.520
what the other person is saying
before you can act upon it.

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:38.360
So it's important to
get that language.

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:39.960
I think you were
very clear about that

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.720
in other ways, communication
as far as respect and listening

00:28:43.720 --> 00:28:46.000
and so forth and whatnot,
what the director wants,

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:47.720
and how you can interpret it.

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:50.480
One way or the other,
you are extremely clear.

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:53.600
What-- a young person,
yourself or whatever--

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:57.440
what advice would you give
them about wanting to be a DP,

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:59.840
or just wanting to be in film?

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:02.800
So the main piece of
advice I would give

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:06.280
is that anything is possible.

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:10.680
If you want to, if you
really want to be in film,

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:12.960
you can be in film.

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:20.600
It takes a focus, a
dedication, a real stick-to-it.

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:24.000
I got my first paying
job as a cameraman.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:26.600
I was probably 19 years old.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:28.400
I shot for 20-odd years.

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:32.240
I had my first feature
film at the age of 39.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:33.520
Wow.

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:37.520
So it doesn't happen overnight.

00:29:37.520 --> 00:29:40.000
And all that time,
I was learning,

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:43.520
learning the basic
skills that are required.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:46.880
So the thing is, don't expect
it to happen overnight.

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:48.080
Just keep at it.

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:48.880
Keep working.

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:49.960
Keep learning.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:52.400
Every job, learn something new.

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:56.120
And just keep building
your skill levels

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:59.400
so that when a
door opens for you,

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:06.800
you can boldly walk through
it and kind of fool people

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:12.800
into thinking that what you know
and create that opportunity.

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:14.960
But it is about
opportunity, and it's

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:16.560
making those opportunities.

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:18.760
And you can only make
those opportunities

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:24.960
by putting yourself in places
with the knowledge that

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:27.840
will allow you to take
advantage of those opportunities

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:30.080
when they're presented with you.

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:31.920
So I think the
thing I would say,

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:36.320
if you want to be a
cinematographer, then shoot.

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.280
Shoot, shoot, shoot.

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:38.960
Shoot.

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:41.480
That's your most basic skill.

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:42.840
Just keep doing that.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:45.720
And look at everything you
shoot and be as critical

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:50.080
as you can about
every single shot.

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:54.120
And think, how can
I do this better?

00:30:54.120 --> 00:30:59.240
And just to that day,
that's what I do.

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:01.480
Every single shot,
I'll look at it

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:04.040
and try and think, well,
how can this be better?

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:07.240
How can this be better?

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:13.480
But a lot of people
want to be in films.

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:16.360
But they don't know what
they want to do in films.

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:18.800
It's very unusual
for a young person

00:31:18.800 --> 00:31:22.760
to even know what a
cinematographer is.

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:30.200
So even if you're in your middle
age, as I was heading towards,

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:32.560
it could still happen.

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:34.720
You just need the
desire and the drive,

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:40.440
and a big, big lot of luck
to get that first door open.

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:42.440
Well, also, you
make your own luck.

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:44.320
You make your own
luck, for sure.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:47.960
And you have done on
multiple occasions.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:50.360
Can you talk to us about art
direction, art directors,

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:52.400
and the collaboration with
you and art directors?

00:31:56.360 --> 00:32:00.600
I have a photograph of you and
Adam Stockhausen somewhere,

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:02.400
the A team.

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:04.880
Yeah, when we did
Widows, that was amazing,

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:05.680
that collaboration.

00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:07.920
And of course, we worked,
and you guys worked together

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:08.800
on 12 Years as well.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:12.280
Tell me about your relationship
with art directors in general.

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:16.600
Well, it's absolutely crucial
to try to develop a relationship

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:18.000
with the art director.

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:21.480
They come on the job months
before the cinematographer

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:22.280
does.

00:32:22.280 --> 00:32:26.360
So they have had an awful lot of
time already with the director.

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:29.560
So the good ones, they
know which direction

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:30.640
they're going in.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:33.920
And they can help
you then in letting

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:36.560
you know what that
direction is initially.

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:38.800
So you get a lift up.

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:43.080
By the time you spend with
them, the closer to the film

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:46.640
you can become, because they're
already that much closer to it.

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:52.720
And then once you start to get
the feel of the film itself,

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:55.480
then the collaboration
comes in, because you're

00:32:55.480 --> 00:32:58.560
able to then suggest
things as well.

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:00.760
And with the two of
you, in the same way

00:33:00.760 --> 00:33:05.320
that you're working with the
director, you can have ideas.

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:08.920
And they will-- the
good ones will listen,

00:33:08.920 --> 00:33:10.640
and then very politely say no.

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:16.240
And the others
won't listen at all.

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:20.240
But it is that
collaborative approach,

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:23.080
which makes it more exciting.

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:24.280
I would never--

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:26.840
I would never begin
to think that I could

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:28.440
do the job of an art director.

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:34.080
 Cinematography is hard
enough, but the skills

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:38.480
you need as an art director
are astounding and so far out

00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:41.120
of my ballpark.

00:33:41.120 --> 00:33:44.760
But talking with
an art director--

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.440
I mean, composition is
one of the key elements

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:48.880
of cinematography.

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:52.120
And as the operator
of the camera,

00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:55.120
I'm looking for
compositions within a set.

00:33:55.120 --> 00:34:00.120
If there's nothing there to work
with, then I'm kind of lost.

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:02.160
All I've got is a face.

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:06.360
So it's always of
interest to understand

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:08.520
what the art
department are doing

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:10.120
and how they're going
to dress the set

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:14.040
and how the set is constructed,
because that then feeds back

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:18.160
into how the compositions
can work within that set.

00:34:18.160 --> 00:34:22.360
So it's a crucial relationship.

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:25.560
Well, we have been
working together now--

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:27.560
we've known each other
as a working relationship

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:29.840
for 20 years.

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:34.600
I think it was 21 years-- no,
20 years ago this month, we

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:41.600
were in Johannesburg in a
mine called Western Deep

00:34:41.600 --> 00:34:45.360
3 kilometers underground
with a Super 8 camera.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:49.560
And our last collaboration
was Widows, a major feature

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.960
film on 35 millimeter.

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:55.320
I'm sorry, but
there you have it.

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:57.480
We've come a long way, Steve.

00:34:57.480 --> 00:34:58.640
No, we have come a long way.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:00.480
You've come a way long way.

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:03.840
But I feel that sort of--
with you, that sort of--

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:05.560
not with me necessarily,
but I'm just

00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:07.800
talking about giving our
relationship an example,

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:10.920
from the six Super 8 to
35, major feature film,

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:12.040
really smart kind of stuff.

00:35:15.200 --> 00:35:16.800
How you're talking
about things is

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:22.280
so tangible and
graspable because it

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:25.200
seems that you've
had a lot of faith

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:29.320
in yourself and in the process.

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:34.520
And it's like-- again,
maybe it's like fishing.

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:36.480
There's an element--
there's a huge element.

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:38.280
There's a chasm of trust.

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:40.760
And it's like, wow, I thought
there would be a bridge there,

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:42.200
but there's not,
but you're still

00:35:42.200 --> 00:35:43.880
able to do what you want to do.

00:35:43.880 --> 00:35:45.480
Where does that come from?

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:53.480
I always talk to you about -
Sean Bobbitt is a Tai Chi DP.

00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:55.880
I always said that
somehow you use the wind

00:35:55.880 --> 00:35:58.400
or use the elements which
are around and you--

00:35:58.400 --> 00:35:59.440
I don't know what you do.

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:02.680
But there's a certain
sense of faith.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:03.800
I don't know.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:04.680
Maybe it's spiritual.

00:36:04.680 --> 00:36:08.040
I don't know, but it's
how do you professionalize

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:09.200
that, I don't know.

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:11.840
How do you do that?

00:36:11.840 --> 00:36:13.680
I don't know.

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:17.200
I'm so fortunate in
the opportunities

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:19.520
that I've been given.

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:23.000
I'm always grateful
for the opportunity.

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:25.400
I'm very grateful to have
worked with you, Steve.

00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:28.880
Western Deep changed
me for life--

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:30.600
some of it good, some
of the not so good.

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:33.880
[INAUDIBLE]

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:36.520
Claustrophobia, oh, my God.

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:37.040
Yikes.

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:40.480
But anyway, it opened
up inside of me

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:47.400
a possibility that I didn't know
existed, which was just that I

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:51.120
could be associated with art.

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:51.800
And see, guys?

00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:53.880
You can do anything,
even with a Picso camera.

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:55.800
So forget your fancy camera.

00:36:55.800 --> 00:36:56.640
You can do anything.

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:57.160
Yeah.

00:36:57.160 --> 00:37:00.320
I want to address the questions
that came from the students,

00:37:00.320 --> 00:37:04.120
because there's a few of them.

00:37:04.120 --> 00:37:06.800
They're keen, and they want
to ask you, Sean Bobbitt,

00:37:06.800 --> 00:37:08.360
the legend, questions.

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:11.320
So the first question is, how
can I learn cinematography

00:37:11.320 --> 00:37:14.360
without a professional camera?

00:37:14.360 --> 00:37:17.400
OK, this is a really
good question.

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:21.280
And it's one that I hope
everyone takes to heart.

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:29.080
A camera is simply a light-tight
box with an aperture for a lens

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:32.280
and a means of
recording the image.

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:33.800
That's it.

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:37.720
It doesn't do and
shouldn't do anything else.

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:42.680
You should be in charge of
what goes through that lens

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:46.240
into the camera.

00:37:46.240 --> 00:37:47.720
People get caught
up in this idea

00:37:47.720 --> 00:37:49.240
that if you get
better camera, you'll

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:50.960
be a better cinematographer.

00:37:50.960 --> 00:37:52.480
It's not true.

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:56.480
It's complete-- it takes
you down a wrong road,

00:37:56.480 --> 00:38:00.040
so far down a wrong road,
that it's something that I

00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:02.920
constantly wish to dispel.

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:05.240
You can make a great movie--

00:38:05.240 --> 00:38:07.400
you can tell a great story--

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:09.280
on an iPhone.

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:12.480
You can tell a story on
whatever type of camera

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:15.160
you have in front of you.

00:38:15.160 --> 00:38:19.320
It's important if you want to
be a working cinematographer

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:22.240
in this huge industry,
you need to understand

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:24.640
the basics of photography.

00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:30.120
Now, cinematographer,
photography is part of it.

00:38:30.120 --> 00:38:34.120
"Cinema" is "motion." it's
ancient Greek for "motion."

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:37.520
so you are working
with moving images.

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:40.000
So once you learn
basic photography,

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:43.200
you then have to transpose
that into the movement,

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:44.960
so that instead of
taking a single shot,

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:48.480
you're working with a series
of shots to create a sequence.

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:53.000
Now, if you don't know
basic photography,

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:57.320
then you will never get beyond
that really basic level.

00:38:57.320 --> 00:38:59.040
The problem with
most of the cameras

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:03.000
today is that they try
to do too much for you.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:07.480
If you turn on auto-focus, if
you turn on auto-iris, if you

00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:10.840
turn on auto-white
balance, the camera's

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:13.200
making all those decisions.

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:16.080
Those are the decisions that
you as a cinematographer

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:19.000
have to make to
create the image.

00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:21.240
So turn all that stuff off.

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:24.200
You need to be in control of
the camera, not the camera

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:25.560
in control of you.

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:29.560
A really high-spec,
professional camera,

00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:34.800
for the cinematographer, it
simply has an on/off button.

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:36.560
And that's it.

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:39.320
There are no bells and
whistles, no frills.

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:43.240
Everything else is down
to you and your crew

00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:49.520
to use the camera to create the
image that you want to create.

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:51.480
But you can't do that
until you understand

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:57.840
the fundamentals of photography,
lighting, and camera movement.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:00.080
That moves into the
second question, really.

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:03.840
The question is, what is your
favorite camera and lenses

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:06.320
to use on set, and why?

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:11.560
Yeah, now I'm just going
to blow it completely.

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:12.640
Exactly.

00:40:14.080 --> 00:40:16.440
No, my favorite camera
to use on the film

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:20.280
is the camera that's
correct for that project.

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:22.480
These days, you have
an immense choice

00:40:22.480 --> 00:40:28.920
between film cameras, Super 8,
Super 16, 35 mil still, 65 mil.

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:31.040
Plus you've got all
the digital formats.

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:32.480
As a cinematographer,
you've never

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:36.120
had such a choice ever in the
history of cinematography.

00:40:36.120 --> 00:40:39.360
And you have lenses, thousands
of different types of lenses.

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:42.440
And the lens is actually more
important than the camera

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:44.520
because that's what
creates the image.

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:45.960
The camera just records it.

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.520
The lens, that's the key.

00:40:48.520 --> 00:40:51.000
So I will choose
the camera and I

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:55.120
will choose the lens in
relation to what the story is.

00:40:55.120 --> 00:40:57.640
For example, 12
Years a Slave, that

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:04.320
had to be shot on 35 mil and
a modern 35 mil film stock,

00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:06.840
for a whole bunch of
different reasons.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:11.080
And part of it was
just the contrast ratio

00:41:11.080 --> 00:41:13.840
that's inherent
in 35 mil film, so

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:16.960
that we can have darker flesh
tones and lighter flesh tones

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:20.720
in the same frame without having
to light them individually,

00:41:20.720 --> 00:41:23.480
so that you can
maintain a reality.

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:27.400
But it also-- film, because
of the grain structure,

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:30.760
it has a sense of period
to it automatically.

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:34.720
So we didn't have to screw
around with things visually

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:36.280
to create that sense of period.

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:38.200
Most of that done was
by the art design.

00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:43.280
But also, subconsciously,
we could induce a past

00:41:43.280 --> 00:41:46.840
by the introduction of the
grain of the film itself.

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:50.760
For this last film I did,
Judas and the Black Messiah,

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.000
it was similar sort of thing.

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:55.480
It's a historical, a
true, historical story.

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:58.680
But given the
location we were at

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:01.960
and various other
financial considerations,

00:42:01.960 --> 00:42:04.240
we had to shoot it digitally.

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:08.840
And so my choice was the
ALEXA LF, the ARRI ALEXA LF,

00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:12.600
large-format camera with
the ARRI DNA lenses--

00:42:12.600 --> 00:42:16.280
large format because, again,
it had that extended contrast

00:42:16.280 --> 00:42:20.080
ratio for darker and
lighter flesh tones,

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:23.840
and the DNA lenses because
the kind of anamorphic look

00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:25.400
to them, although
they're spherical.

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:28.560
So again, you get
that period feel,

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:29.880
but it's a subconscious thing.

00:42:29.880 --> 00:42:31.720
It's not in your face.

00:42:31.720 --> 00:42:34.320
So for example, going
down in Western Deep,

00:42:34.320 --> 00:42:37.520
Super 8, little cassettes--

00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:38.520
why?

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:40.440
Because it was the
only camera that we

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:45.600
could take in terms of safety,
but also in terms of size

00:42:45.600 --> 00:42:47.520
and ease of use.

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:51.920
So the camera, you
choose what works.

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:52.600
OK.

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:54.360
The next question
is-- and I think

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:56.680
it relates to what you just
said before-- which film

00:42:56.680 --> 00:42:58.520
or films in your
career helped you

00:42:58.520 --> 00:43:00.320
to find your voice
as a cinematographer?

00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:03.080
What did you learn
from those experiences?

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:04.680
I think you answered
that question, no?

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:08.640
Yeah, and every film
informs every other film

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:11.160
that you ever do.

00:43:11.160 --> 00:43:13.520
For an actor, the
image is everything.

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:19.040
So what is the cinematographer's
relationship with the actors

00:43:19.040 --> 00:43:20.400
like?

00:43:20.400 --> 00:43:23.640
Well, again, as I said
before, it's respect.

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:26.640
But there's also one
other very crucial thing

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:30.360
to be aware of as a
cinematographer, and especially

00:43:30.360 --> 00:43:31.280
if you're an operator.

00:43:31.280 --> 00:43:33.840
If you're there with
the actors and you're

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:38.800
right up next to them, the
director is some distance away.

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:42.960
So at the end of every scene,
the actors look at you first.

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:43.840
They do.

00:43:43.840 --> 00:43:44.440
They do.

00:43:44.440 --> 00:43:47.440
And you cannot
interact with them.

00:43:47.440 --> 00:43:51.240
You must never get in the
way in between an actor

00:43:51.240 --> 00:43:52.400
and a director.

00:43:52.400 --> 00:43:54.120
You don't give direction.

00:43:54.120 --> 00:43:56.400
You don't tell them
it's good or it's bad.

00:43:56.400 --> 00:43:59.800
I just smile like an idiot
and kind of nod my head

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:03.520
and turn around and walk
away, because that's not

00:44:03.520 --> 00:44:07.200
my job to get involved.

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:09.520
And if you do start
to get involved,

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:14.280
that is a very rocky road
that's going to lead to tears.

00:44:14.280 --> 00:44:16.360
Thanks.

00:44:16.360 --> 00:44:16.960
Yeah.

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:17.720
Yeah.

00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:21.120
Well, I have said--

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:23.760
I have actually said,
Sean, what do you think?

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:26.280
At times, yeah.

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:27.400
That-- it's allowed.

00:44:27.400 --> 00:44:28.840
And I'll always have an opinion.

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:30.160
Yes, and thank God.

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:32.480
Thank God for it.

00:44:32.480 --> 00:44:35.000
The last question--

00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:39.120
Lupita Nyong'o is admired
and highly respected by young

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:42.160
people in Kenya and
in the refugee camps.

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:44.320
What was it like
to work with her?

00:44:44.320 --> 00:44:47.640
What is she like as
an artist on set?

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:51.000
And I was aware of
the incredible journey

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:55.080
you'd been on to find
just the right person.

00:44:55.080 --> 00:45:00.840
And so I was really intrigued
as to who this person was.

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:06.680
And there is a focus
and a calmness to her.

00:45:06.680 --> 00:45:09.880
And I've had the pleasure
of working with her again

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:11.800
on Queen of Katwe.

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:15.280
And it is boom.

00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:20.640
When she comes on to
set, she's ready to go.

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:22.160
She knows her stuff.

00:45:22.160 --> 00:45:24.080
She's done all the work.

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:31.320
And it's really-- it's always
a pleasure just to watch,

00:45:31.320 --> 00:45:34.400
because there is stuff that's
going to happen there that you

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:36.160
never expected to find.

00:45:36.160 --> 00:45:40.240
And there's such a
professionalism as well.

00:45:40.240 --> 00:45:44.080
And in terms of the
relationship with the director,

00:45:44.080 --> 00:45:50.000
with everyone else, she's
a fantastic role model--

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:50.600
And she was--

00:45:50.600 --> 00:45:54.160
--for anyone who's interested
in getting into cinema.

00:45:54.160 --> 00:45:56.080
She was a star,
for sure, because I

00:45:56.080 --> 00:45:59.080
think it was her star
that shined brightly,

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:01.320
but it was one of
those things where

00:46:01.320 --> 00:46:04.120
she was right for
the role, but also

00:46:04.120 --> 00:46:08.320
right for the moment, and
her speeches at that time,

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:10.120
and when we're hearing
about people's skin

00:46:10.120 --> 00:46:13.240
color in the press today.

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:16.240
And I remember Lupita
giving this amazing speech.

00:46:16.240 --> 00:46:19.240
I think it was at the--

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:22.560
it was the Essence Awards
when she talked about her skin

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:25.360
color, being so dark and
how she wanted to be light.

00:46:25.360 --> 00:46:27.440
And it was just--

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:29.360
she was just amazing.

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:30.160
That's a star.

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:32.720
That's a star.

00:46:32.720 --> 00:46:38.440
OK, that was the last
question from the students.

00:46:38.440 --> 00:46:40.840
And thank you very much
for those questions.

00:46:40.840 --> 00:46:44.280
And Sean, it's a
pleasure knowing you,

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:45.520
an honor knowing you.

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:47.040
You are an artist.

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:47.840
I'll say it again.

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:50.080
You are an artist.

00:46:50.080 --> 00:46:51.520
If you didn't know
it-- better you

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:54.800
don't know it so your
head doesn't get that big.

00:46:54.800 --> 00:46:55.520
But--

00:46:55.520 --> 00:46:57.520
I still don't believe
you, but it's nice of you.

00:46:57.520 --> 00:46:58.840
Well, regardless.

00:46:58.840 --> 00:47:00.360
OK, well, trust me.

00:47:00.360 --> 00:47:05.240
But you're an artist
and an amazing DP.

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:08.240
And I just want to say thank
you for all of your amazing work

00:47:08.240 --> 00:47:10.760
through working with all
these directors and the images

00:47:10.760 --> 00:47:11.240
you've made.

00:47:11.240 --> 00:47:14.920
And Judas and the Black Messiah
is an amazing piece of work,

00:47:14.920 --> 00:47:16.080
so congratulations on that.

00:47:16.080 --> 00:47:18.480
And thank you very
much, everyone,

00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:21.120
for tuning into this
conversation with Sean Bobbitt.

00:47:21.120 --> 00:47:22.360
Sean, thank you.

00:47:22.360 --> 00:47:23.600
And everyone else, good night.

00:47:23.600 --> 00:47:24.280
Take care.

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:24.560
Bye bye.

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:25.280
Thank you, Steve.

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:26.240
It's been a real pleasure.

